\begin{abstract}
In a foundational work, Bruinier and Ono established $p$-adic divisibility properties for the Fourier coefficients of half-integral weight cusp forms $f(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a(n) q^n \in S_{\lambda+1/2}(\Gamma_0(N), \chi) \cap \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$, with particular emphasis on small primes $p \in \{2, 3\}$ or restrictive hypotheses on the level $N$. In this paper, we extend the Bruinier--Ono framework to encompass all primes $p \ge 5$. By leveraging the Shimura correspondence alongside Serre's theory of $p$-adic Galois representations and modular forms modulo $p$, we prove that for any $m \ge 1$ and any prime $p \ge 5$, the Fourier coefficients $a(n)$ satisfy
\[
a(n) \equiv 0 \pmod{p^m}
\]
for a subset of integers $n$ of natural density one. Furthermore, we establish sub-convex bounds on the distribution of the first non-vanishing coefficients modulo $p^m$ and discuss implications for the arithmetic of traces of singular moduli and generalized partition functions.
\end{abstract}

\end{document}
